# Microsoft Backbone Infrastructure Provider

Microsoft Backbone Network is Microsoft's global private fiber network infrastructure.

Azure traffic travels through:
- Microsoft-owned data centers
- Fiber optic cables
- Global private network

instead of public internet.

---

# Why It Is Important

Benefits:
- High speed
- Low latency
- Secure communication
- Global connectivity
- Better reliability

---

# Example

User accesses Azure app.

Traffic flow:

User
   |
Nearest Microsoft Edge Location
   |
Microsoft Backbone Network
   |
Azure Data Center

Traffic stays inside Microsoft network as much as possible.

---

# Real World Example

India User
   |
Chennai Edge POP
   |
Microsoft Backbone
   |
Singapore Azure Region

Instead of:
Public internet routing.

---

# How Data Flow Happens from Layer 1 to Layer 7

OSI Model:

| Layer | Name |
|------|------|
| 7 | Application |
| 6 | Presentation |
| 5 | Session |
| 4 | Transport |
| 3 | Network |
| 2 | Data Link |
| 1 | Physical |

---

# Example:
User opens:
https://example.com

---

# Layer 7 → Application Layer

Protocol:
HTTP/HTTPS

Browser creates request.

Example:
GET /index.html

---

# Layer 6 → Presentation Layer

Handles:
- Encryption
- SSL/TLS
- Data formatting

HTTPS encryption happens here.

---

# Layer 5 → Session Layer

Creates and maintains communication session.

Example:
- Session cookies
- Authentication session

---

# Layer 4 → Transport Layer

Protocol:
TCP/UDP

TCP:
- Reliable communication
- Packet sequencing
- Retransmission

Port Examples:
- 80
- 443

Data divided into segments.

---

# Layer 3 → Network Layer

Handles:
- IP addressing
- Routing

Example:
Source IP:
192.168.1.10

Destination IP:
20.x.x.x

Routers decide path.

---

# Layer 2 → Data Link Layer

Handles:
- MAC Address
- Switching
- VLAN

Switch forwards frames inside LAN.

Example:
MAC:
00:1A:2B:XX:YY

---

# Layer 1 → Physical Layer

Actual transmission:
- Electrical signals
- Fiber optics
- Wireless signals

Data travels physically.

---

# Complete Data Flow

Application Data
      ↓
Encryption
      ↓
TCP Segments
      ↓
IP Packets
      ↓
Ethernet Frames
      ↓
Electrical/Fiber Signals

Receiver processes reverse order.

---

# VLAN (Virtual LAN)

VLAN = Logical segmentation inside Layer 2 network.

It divides one physical switch into multiple virtual networks.

---

# Why VLAN?

Benefits:
- Isolation
- Security
- Reduced broadcast traffic
- Better management

---

# VLAN Example

Single Switch:

VLAN 10 → HR
VLAN 20 → Finance
VLAN 30 → IT

Devices inside different VLANs cannot communicate directly.

---

# VLAN Working at Layer 2

Layer 2 switch uses:
- MAC Address
- VLAN Tags

Each frame gets:
- VLAN ID

Example:
VLAN 10

Switch forwards traffic only inside same VLAN.

---

# Example

PC1 → VLAN 10
PC2 → VLAN 10

Communication allowed.

PC1 → VLAN 10
PC3 → VLAN 20

Communication blocked at Layer 2.

---

# VLAN Tagging

IEEE Standard:
802.1Q

Adds VLAN ID into Ethernet frame.

---

# Layer 3 VLAN Communication

Different VLANs require:
- Router
OR
- Layer 3 Switch

This is called:
Inter-VLAN Routing

---

# Inter-VLAN Routing Example

VLAN 10:
192.168.10.0/24

VLAN 20:
192.168.20.0/24

Layer 3 switch routes traffic between VLANs.

---

# Layer 2 vs Layer 3

| Layer 2 | Layer 3 |
|----------|----------|
| MAC Address | IP Address |
| Switching | Routing |
| VLAN | Inter-VLAN Routing |
| Same Network | Different Networks |

---

# Real Enterprise Architecture

Users
   |
